  <p>
    Somewhere in a warehouse right now, a worker is unpacking a dress that fit perfectly.
    On the size chart, anyway. In the fitting-room mirror of the buyer's bedroom, it didn't.
    Back in the box it went, joining an estimated <strong>30–40% of all clothing bought online</strong>
    that makes the return journey. For in-store purchases, that figure is under 10%. The
    difference isn't buyer's remorse. It's that when we shop online, we're asked to guess,
    and the game is rigged against us.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">The vanity sizing spiral</h2>
  <p>
    In 1958, the US government published a voluntary commercial standard for women's
    clothing sizes, giving each size number a defined set of body measurements. It was
    revised in 1970 and withdrawn entirely in 1983, by which point most brands had long
    since stopped following it. In the decades since, sizes have drifted steadily downward
    in a phenomenon researchers call vanity sizing: as average bodies grew, the numbers on
    the labels shrank to flatter us. A women's size 12 from 1958 is roughly a size 4–6
    today. The label stopped being a measurement and became a marketing decision.
  </p>
  <p>
    The result: a "medium" is not a fact about a garment. It's a brand's opinion about who
    they want wearing it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Every brand speaks its own language</h2>
  <p>
    Even setting vanity sizing aside, brands genuinely cut for different bodies. Zara
    designs to a slim continental European block (narrower shoulders, shorter torso). Nike
    cuts athletic and runs footwear about half a size small. American heritage brands cut
    generously through the chest and seat. None of them is wrong; they're just designing
    for different people while using the same size labels.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the shopper who is a confident M at H&amp;M orders an M at Zara and gets something
    they can't button. They conclude, reasonably but wrongly, that their body is the
    problem. Their body was never the problem. The label was.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Size chart chaos within a single brand</h2>
  <p>
    Here's the part that surprises even seasoned shoppers: the same brand often doesn't fit
    like itself. A brand's denim line, its youth line, and its main collection are
    frequently developed on different fit blocks, in different factories, on different
    continents. H&amp;M's Divided line famously runs a full size smaller than its basics.
    Two "size 10" dresses from the same retailer can differ by more than an inch in the
    bust, well within normal manufacturing tolerance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Size charts, meanwhile, describe the <em>body</em> the garment was designed for, not
    the garment itself, and they rarely mention ease, stretch, or cut. A chart can be
    technically accurate and still lead you to the wrong size.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">What all those returns actually cost</h2>
  <p>
    Returns feel free, but somebody pays. Retailers spend an estimated $20–$30 to process a
    single returned garment: shipping, inspection, repackaging, restocking. Industry
    analyses put the annual cost of returns in the hundreds of billions of dollars, and a
    significant share of returned clothing is never resold at all: it's landfilled or
    incinerated because processing it costs more than it's worth. The environmental bill
    (double shipping, packaging waste, destroyed inventory) lands on all of us.
  </p>
  <p>
    And shoppers pay twice: once in the prices that absorb all that processing, and again
    in the quiet tax of time: ordering three sizes to try at home, queueing at the drop-off
    point, waiting on refunds.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">The fix isn't better size charts. It's no size charts.</h2>
  <p>
    Every failure above has the same root cause. A size label is a lossy, brand-specific
    compression of the only data that matters: the measurements of the garment and the
    measurements of you.
  </p>
  <p>
    That's the premise we built <a href="/app/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline-offset-2 hover:underline">Attirel</a> on.
    You measure yourself once, with a guided tutorial. Attirel compares your actual
